      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-221775",
      ":question/problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0$ for any $n\\in N.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n11n-{n}^{2}-28{{field-4}}0.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n-{n}^{2}-28 & =11({{field-6}})-({{field-7}})^{2}-28 \\\\  & ={{field-8}} \\\\  & {{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-10}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."
    },
