      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-221834",
      ":question/problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\n${x}^{2}+5x+6\\ge 0$ for any $x\\in R.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+6{{field-4}}0.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+6 & ={({{field-6}})}^{2}+5({{field-7}})+6 \\\\  & ={{field-8}} \\\\  & {{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=$ {{field-10}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."
    }
